    ns1:methodologyContext [ ns1:framPrinciples [ rdfs:comment """People - and organisations - have to adjust what they do to match 
the actual conditions, resources and demands. Since resources and time are finite, 
such adjustments will always be approximate. Because the adjustments are approximate, 
they will also be variable - and this variability is essential for safety.""" ],
                [ rdfs:comment """Functional resonance is defined as the detectable signal that emerges 
from the unintended interaction of the variability of many functions. Functional resonance 
can lead to both positive and negative outcomes. The resonance is not caused by failures 
or malfunctions, but by the normal variability of functions.""" ],
                [ rdfs:comment """Things that go right and things that go wrong happen in basically 
the same way. Adverse outcomes (accidents, incidents) happen because something 
goes wrong, i.e., because of malfunctions, failures, or errors. Things that go 
well happen because the performance, or the functions, match the demands, 
i.e., because functions do what they are supposed to do.""" ],
                [ rdfs:comment """Both wanted and unwanted outcomes are emergent phenomena, arising 
from the variability of everyday performance - also called normal performance 
variability. The variability of multiple functions may combine in unexpected ways, 
leading to disproportionately large consequences. This is called functional resonance.""" ] ] ;
    ns1:scenarioCount 4 ;
    ns1:scenariosEnabled true ;
    ns1:summary [ ns1:averageConnectionsPerFunction 3.4e+00 ;
            ns1:couplingCount 34 ;
            ns1:couplingsByAspect [ ] ;
            ns1:functionCount 20 ;
            ns1:functionsByType [ ] ;
            ns1:maxConnectionsFunction "M11-Train dispatch control" ] ;
